## Academic writing and terminology discipline

- Define a nontrivial construct when it first carries argumentative weight. State what it is in this study, why it matters to the problem or mechanism, and its stated scope; do not insert a detached glossary or dictionary-style definition.
- Expand every non-obvious acronym at its first occurrence in each researcher-facing document. In Chinese prose, use `Full English Name（简洁中文释义，ABBR）` only when `ABBR` is a real, stable abbreviation that will recur; in English prose, use `Full English Name (ABBR)`. Later mentions may use the stable abbreviation. Never introduce an acronym merely to shorten a phrase, and never expect a reader to infer an acronym from a component ID such as `COMP1`.
- Keep a canonical term, its Chinese explanation, and any stable acronym consistent across the blueprint, claim registry, hypotheses, experiment plan, and Proposal. A component or claim ID supports traceability but never replaces its researcher-facing name and explanation.
- Explain a named method, condition, metric, baseline family, or theoretical construct at first use only to the extent supported by the current materials. Do not turn a label into an invented formal definition, empirical finding, or literature-wide consensus.
- Use connected academic prose. Every paragraph must advance one claim, comparison, mechanism, design choice, evaluation decision, boundary, or implication and connect to the next paragraph. Do not use a stack of labels, a glossary dump, unexplained abbreviations, generic AI filler, or repeated “this study” assertions to create apparent depth.
- Before `finish_task`, re-read `hypotheses.md` and `research_proposal.md` as a new domain reader: confirm that titles, central constructs, acronyms, components, claims, baselines, metrics, and evaluation conditions are introduced before later shorthand is used.
- Final validation uses two layers: deterministic checks govern schema, source lineage, claim-to-experiment mappings, evidence boundaries, anti-repetition, and internal-audit-language leaks; an independent LLM then batch-reviews only remaining prose-semantic ambiguities. When `validate_t45_research_package` reports `semantic_review_candidates`, address all of them together if the argument is actually incomplete. If the current prose already satisfies one naturally (for example through Chinese academic phrasing rather than an English label), preserve the explicit passage: the independent reviewer can accept it only with verbatim quotes tied to the unchanged source files.

## Proposal prose standard

Write the Proposal to the standard of a strong UTD/FT50 IS or CCF-A research proposal, adapted to the selected orientation. The argument should move naturally from a real-world information or decision problem, to the computational difficulty created by existing assumptions, to the technical design that is necessary to resolve that difficulty. Do not introduce a method first and retrofit motivation afterward.

Use complete, connected paragraphs rather than a stack of short claims, field labels, or checklists. Make the transition from literature to gap, from gap to challenge, from challenge to design, and from design to evaluation explicit in the prose. Prefer precise verbs and concrete logical connections over abstract slogans, defensive disclaimers, or repeated statements of importance. Avoid excessive colons, dashes, semicolons, fragments, and itemized micro-sentences; headings are the only structural signposts required. Keep the document compact without compressing away design rationale, the development of the literature gap, or the link between technical and practical significance.

